{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And if his means do not reach.</b> They do not say to him: borrow, and they do not say to him: engage in your trade. If he has a lamb (and has not) [that] and does not have his needs — from where do we know that he brings a [poor person's] offering? Scripture says: די שה (sufficient for a lamb)." "<b>One for a sin-offering and one for a burnt-offering.</b> Since the bird sin-offering gives the altar nothing but its blood, which is not an eating — therefore he must bring two: one as a sin-offering for the eating of the priest, and one as a burnt-offering for the eating of the altar. But when he is wealthy and obligated in an animal sin-offering, a lamb or a goat suffices, because from it there is a fitting eating for the priest and the fats are an eating for the altar."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "The text as transmitted contains a confused bracketed variant '(ואין לו) [שה]'; translated hyper-literally as '(and has not) [that]'. The logic appears to be: if he does not have a lamb and does not have his basic needs met, whence do we know he brings a reduced offering? From 'די שה'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:10:06.052402596Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}